  <h1>Senior dog nutrition: feeding the years that matter most</h1>
  <p class="guide-lede">"She's just slowing down" is half true. Age is real — but a lot of what we file under old age is actually weight, joints, and a bowl that never changed after year two. The last third of a dog's life is the part you'll remember most. Feed it that way.</p>

  <h2>When is a dog "senior"?</h2>
  <p>Sooner for big dogs, later for small ones: giant breeds around 6–7, large breeds around 7–8, small breeds closer to 10. The calendar matters less than the signals — graying muzzle, slower stairs, longer naps, pickier appetite. Any of those is the cue to look at the bowl again.</p>

  <h2>Do senior dogs need less protein?</h2>
  <p>This is the most damaging myth in senior feeding — the answer for most dogs is <strong>more, not less</strong>. Aging dogs lose muscle, and muscle is what keeps them standing, climbing, and steady. Quality protein preserves it. The old "protein strains old kidneys" idea doesn't hold for healthy dogs; only dogs with diagnosed kidney disease need managed protein, and that's a vet-prescribed diet, not a shelf decision.</p>

  <h2>What should change in the bowl?</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Calories down, protein up.</strong> Seniors move less and burn less, but need their muscle more than ever. That combination is exactly what good senior formulas are built for.</li>
    <li><strong>Joint support in the food or beside it.</strong> Omega-3s (EPA/DHA) and glucosamine earn their place from about the senior threshold on — see our <a href="/guides/dog-joint-supplements/">joint supplement guide</a> for what's real.</li>
    <li><strong>Keep them lean — this is the big one.</strong> Lean-fed dogs in long-term research lived meaningfully longer with later arthritis. In the senior years, the rib check (see the <a href="/guides/dog-weight-management/">weight guide</a>) matters more than at any other age.</li>
    <li><strong>Softer, smellier, warmer if appetite fades.</strong> Aging noses dull, and appetite follows. Warm water on the food, a topper, or a switch to wet food often restarts interest before anything is medically wrong.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>My senior is losing weight — is that just age?</h2>
  <p>No. Unplanned weight loss is never "just age" — it's dental pain, kidney or thyroid issues, or something else with a name, until a vet says otherwise. The same for suddenly ravenous, suddenly picky, or drinking noticeably more water. Seniors deserve a lower threshold for the vet call, not a higher one.</p>

  <h2>When is it a vet problem?</h2>
  <p>Beyond the weight rule above: any appetite change lasting more than a couple of days, difficulty chewing, or stiffness that arrived suddenly rather than gradually. And twice-yearly senior checkups are worth it — half the things that shorten senior years are cheap to treat when caught early.</p>

